<h2>5. How to Get Your First 5 Clients</h2>

<h3>Method 1: Instagram Cold DMs (Most Effective in India)</h3>

<p>Find creators or businesses with 5K-50K followers who post video content. Their editing is probably mediocre. Send this DM:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>"Hey [Name], I love your content about [specific topic]. I noticed your last video could really pop with tighter pacing and a quick color grade. I made a 30-second sample edit of your latest video — mind if I send it over? No charge, just wanted to show what's possible. 🎬"</p>
</blockquote>

<p><strong>Key points:</strong> Be specific (not "I love your channel"), offer value first (free sample), and keep it short. I sent 40+ DMs like this when starting out. 6 responded, 3 became paying clients. That's a normal conversion rate.</p>

<h3>Method 2: LinkedIn (Best for Corporate Clients)</h3>
<p>Post your before/after edit comparisons on LinkedIn. Tag it with #VideoEditing #ContentCreation #IndianCreators. Marketing managers at startups actively look for freelance editors on LinkedIn. It takes 2-3 months of consistent posting to see results.</p>

<h3>Method 3: Freelance Platforms</h3>
<p>Create profiles on Upwork and Fiverr once you have 3-5 testimonials. Your first few jobs will be low-paying (₹1,000-3,000 per video). That's fine — you're building reviews. After 10+ five-star reviews, you can raise rates significantly. Use international pricing on Upwork for global clients — $50-150 per YouTube video is standard for intermediate editors.</p>

<h3>Method 4: Local Network</h3>
<p>Talk to photographers, wedding planners, event companies, and real estate agents in your city. Many need video editing but don't have an in-house editor. Offer a trial project at a discounted rate. Word-of-mouth referrals from local professionals are how I got 60% of my early clients at Wedream Production.</p>

