<tr><td><strong>Wedding Highlight (3-5 min)</strong></td><td>₹3,000–₹8,000</td><td>₹10,000–₹25,000</td><td>₹25,000–₹75,000</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Corporate Video (1-3 min)</strong></td><td>₹3,000–₹8,000</td><td>₹10,000–₹30,000</td><td>₹30,000–₹1,00,000+</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Monthly Retainer</strong></td><td>₹10,000–₹25,000</td><td>₹25,000–₹50,000</td><td>₹50,000–₹1,50,000+</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Pricing tip:</strong> Never quote hourly in India — clients will argue about every minute. Quote per project with clear deliverables (number of revisions, delivery timeline, format). I always include "2 rounds of revisions included, additional revisions at ₹X each" in my contracts. Saves countless arguments.
</div>

<h2>7. Red Flags in Client Communication</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>"I'll pay you in exposure"</strong> — Run. Exposure doesn't pay rent.</li>
<li><strong>"Can you do a free trial project first?"</strong> — Counter with "I'll do a 30-second sample, but the full project is paid." If they insist on a full free project, they're not a real client.</li>
<li><strong>"I need this by tomorrow"</strong> (sent at 11 PM) — Charge rush fees (50-100% extra). Urgency has a price.</li>
<li><strong>No written agreement</strong> — Always send a simple email/WhatsApp confirming scope, price, timeline, and revision limits before starting work. Even for ₹2,000 projects. I lost ₹15,000 once because I didn't have a written agreement and the client "forgot" the price we discussed.</li>
<li><strong>"Make it more... you know... cinematic"</strong> — Vague feedback means the client doesn't know what they want. Ask specific questions: "Do you mean warmer colors, slower pacing, or different music?"</li>
</ul>

<h2>FAQ</h2>

<details><summary>Do I need a degree to become a freelance video editor?</summary>
<p>No. Not a single client has ever asked me about my formal education. They look at your portfolio, your communication skills, and your ability to deliver on time. Skills and proof of work matter infinitely more than certificates. See our <a href="/should-you-become-a-video-editor-2026">full career guide</a>.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>How long does it take to get my first paying client?</summary>
<p>If you actively DM creators and post your work online: 2-6 weeks. If you just "put your portfolio out there" and wait: could be months. Active outreach is everything in the beginning. Consistency beats talent when talent doesn't show up.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Should I specialize in one type of editing or be a generalist?</summary>
<p>Specialize as soon as possible. "I edit YouTube videos for tech creators" is 10x more hireable than "I do all kinds of editing." Specialization lets you charge more, build a reputation faster, and attract the right clients. You can always expand later.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>What software should I learn to get hired?</summary>
<p>DaVinci Resolve (free) covers 80% of editing needs. If you want maximum job market coverage, learn Premiere Pro as well — it's still the industry standard for agencies and production houses. See our <a href="/final-cut-pro-vs-premiere-pro-vs-davinci-resolve-2026">NLE comparison guide</a> for details.</p>
</details>

